        <h1>What I learned about silence in <em>the high passes</em>.</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">Three weeks at altitude. No signal. One notebook. An essay on solitude, attention, and the unexpected geography of slowness.</p>
        <div class="article-byline">
          <div class="avatar"></div>
          <div>
            <div class="name">By Editor</div>
            <div class="meta">Published 8 May 2026 · Photographs by Triplipi</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Article body -->
    <article class="section">
      <div class="container-reading">

        <div class="article-body" data-reveal>
          <p class="dropcap">The first morning at altitude, you wake up at four. Not because the light is in, but because your body has decided, on its own, that this is a new ratio of breath to thought. The room is cold. The dog outside is quiet. There is a small thrill in being awake while the world hasn't quite caught up.</p>

          <p>Leh is at 3,500 metres. The flight in from Delhi takes about ninety minutes, and you arrive feeling fine — better than fine — until the first staircase. Then you understand. The air is the same, but there is less of it; what you took for granted has been quietly rationed.</p>

          <p>I went to Ladakh in late September, when the season is ending and the light has the particular gold that only the high desert gets. I went without an itinerary. I had two notebooks, one camera, three rolls of film I never developed, and a vague intention to walk to Hemis Monastery and stop somewhere along the way if a tea shop looked right.</p>

          <h2>The geography of slowness</h2>

          <p>You cannot rush at altitude. This is not a metaphor. The body refuses. You climb a flight of stairs and you have to wait, breath catching up to the act of having taken the stairs, before you can do the next thing. The first day, this feels like an inconvenience. By the third day, it feels like a kind of permission.</p>

          <p>I started writing differently. Not better, necessarily, but slower. Sentences that would have been three sentences became one. I stopped looking at my phone — partly because there was no signal in the villages, partly because the act of looking down at a screen suddenly felt absurd, given what was at eye level.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Bleed figure -->
        <figure class="article-figure is-bleed" data-reveal="scale">
          <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1561361513-2d000a50f0dc?w=2000&q=85" alt="">
          <figcaption>Above Leh, at the turn of the road that leads to Khardung La. The colour the sky becomes at this altitude is its own thing.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="article-body" data-reveal>
          <p>There's a thing that happens in Hemis Monastery, when you arrive at the right hour. The puja is starting. The young monks are filing in, their crimson robes pooling around them. There's a drum, then a horn, then the kind of low chanting that you can feel in your sternum before you can hear it.</p>

          <blockquote class="article-pullquote">
            <p>The first time I felt the chanting in my chest, I realised I had been listening with the wrong organ.</p>
            <cite>— Field note, day 4</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>I sat there for two hours. I did not check my watch. I did not feel restless. When it ended, the head lama looked at the small group of visitors and smiled — at no-one in particular, at all of us — and that smile contained more attention than most conversations I have had.</p>

          <h2>What altitude takes, and what it gives back</h2>

          <p>The first time I felt the chanting in my chest, I realised I had been listening with the wrong organ. We talk about <em>listening</em> as if it were a quality of the ears, but at four thousand metres, with one's heart going a bit faster than usual, it becomes apparent that the whole body listens, given the chance.</p>
        </div>

