        <div class="article-body" data-reveal>
          <p>This is the gift altitude gives you, in exchange for the small rationing of breath: a return to the body. You become aware of your pulse. You become aware of your steps. You become aware, when you sit down somewhere, that you have actually sat down — not collapsed into a chair while continuing some other mental motion.</p>

          <h2>The road to Pangong, and the silence that comes after</h2>

          <p>From Leh, the road to Pangong is one of those drives that begins as a logistical proposition and becomes, by hour four, a sort of pilgrimage. You cross Chang La, the third-highest motorable pass in the world, where prayer flags fly in such density that the wind takes on a sound — a paper sound — that you don't hear anywhere else.</p>

          <p>You descend, then climb, then descend, and at some point the lake comes into view. It is forty miles long. It changes colour roughly every ten minutes. There are no buildings near most of its shoreline. You stop the car, get out, and there is nothing to say.</p>
        </div>

