          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1587474260584-136574528ed5?w=1000&q=80" alt="">
            <figcaption>Prayer flags on the descent.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="article-body" data-reveal>
          <h2>What I brought back</h2>

          <p>I came home with three rolls of film I haven't developed. I came home with two notebooks I am still reading. I came home with the particular ache that high-altitude travel leaves in the soft tissue around your eyes, which lasts for about a week after you return to sea level.</p>

          <p>And I came home with this — the small, lasting suspicion that most of what we call attention is actually just busy-ness wearing a coat. That somewhere at four thousand metres, with the wind moving through the prayer flags and the chanting moving through the body, there is a different, older version of attention that we have largely forgotten how to access.</p>

          <p>It's still there. It's just that you have to climb to get to it.</p>
        </div>

